          body: `A first word rarely sounds like the adult version. The CDC's example at 15 months is "ba" for ball. Great Ormond Street Hospital notes that once children say one or two meaningful words, they tend to use them consistently. What makes it a word is that your child uses the same sound, on purpose, for the same thing or person. "Mama" said only while babbling "mamamama" is not yet a word; "mama" said while reaching for you is.\n\nSigns and gestures are not spoken words, but they count as communication. The CDC lists waving "bye-bye" at 1 year and pointing to ask for something or get help at 15 months.\n\nUnderstanding comes first. Great Ormond Street Hospital notes that between one and two years, children often understand far more than they can say, and that by around two many have a core vocabulary of roughly one to two hundred words and are joining two words together. The spread between children of the same age is large.`,
